  "worked_solution": "To compute the probability density function of a random variable $Y,$ where $Y=u(X)$ is a continuous, strictly monotonic function of a continuous random variable $X,$ we follow two steps:\n\n- First, we find an expression for ${F}_{Y}$ in terms of ${F}_{X},$ where ${F}_{Y}$ and ${F}_{X}$ are the cumulative distribution functions of $Y$ and $X,$ respectively.\n\n- Then, we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ to get the probability density function ${f}_{Y}.$\n\nBefore we start, note that $Y=\\frac{X}{3}$ is a strictly decreasing function of $X.$ Moreover, for $0<x<1,$ we have $0<y<\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14231262/assets/42a3c2510927b66842ec0360eabfb169a306ccaf52292132f31769036e608e69.png)\n\nNow, using the definition of the cumulative distribution of $Y,$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}_{Y}(y) & =P(Y\\le y) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{X}{3}\\le y) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 3y) \\\\  & ={F}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo compute the PDF ${f}_{Y}(y)$ for $0<y<\\frac{1}{3},$ we differentiate ${F}_{Y}$ with respect to $y$ using the chain rule. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & ={F}_{Y}^{′}(y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {F}_{X}^{′}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the definition of ${f}_{X},$ we finally arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{Y}(y) & =3\\cdot {f}_{X}(3y) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(1-(3y)^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for the PDF of $Y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y}(y)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{9}{2}(1-9{y}^{2}), & \\,0<y<\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
